  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1159 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nSunday night through Thursday:\n\nThe east-west surface baroclinic zone is forecast to remain stalled \nover northern IL/IN into Monday, with an approaching 500mb shortwave \nphasing well enough with it to merit some continued chances for \nlight rainfall over southern WI. Our positioning north of the \nsurface boundary ought to limit thunder potential and keep the rain \nshowers relatively light for Monday.\nPredominantly dry weather with only slight chances for rain into \nTuesday as the surface frontal zone sags further south.\nPredominantly dry weather should then continue through midweek as \nhigh pressure remains dominant over the region. \n\nThe aforementioned 500mb trough from Monday may stall nearby and \nclose off into a cutoff upper-low midweek next week, a pattern that \noften throws a wrench into model predictability. That said, models \nhave a stable solution on a strong high pressure system over \nsouthern Ontario Canada early next week, shifting east into Quebec \nmidweek, which leads us to expect a persistent northeast breeze and \ncool weather through the period (highs in the 60s, lows around 50).\n\nAt the very end of our forecast run (later next week), we begin to \nsee ridging in the jet, slowly building east into the Upper Midwest. \nTemperatures should lean above seasonal norms at some point late \nnext week into the subsequent weekend. \n\nSheppard",
